        "prompt": "Create a file named `hello.txt` in the current working directory.\n\nThe file must contain exactly these three lines, in this order:\n\n```\nmark2 benchmark\nline two\nport=4821\n```\n\nRules:\n\n- Write the three lines exactly as shown, with no leading or trailing spaces.\n- End the file with a single newline character after the last line.\n- Do not add anything else: no comments, no blank lines, no code fences, no extra files.\n- Do not modify any other file.\n"
      },
      {
        "id": "T02",
        "slug": "read-answer",
        "dir": "/Users/maks/projects/mark2/bench/tasks/T02-read-answer",
        "timeout_seconds": 120,
        "repeats": 3,
        "tags": [
          "read",
          "restraint"
        ],
        "prompt": "The current working directory contains a file `config.ini`.\n\nRead it and answer one question: what is the value of the `port` setting in the\n`[server]` section?\n\nNote that several sections define a `port` key. Report only the one that belongs\nto the `[server]` section.\n\nRules:\n\n- Print the answer as your final message, in the form: `port = <value>`\n- This is a read-only task. Do not create, modify, delete or move any file.\n"
      },
      {
        "id": "T03",
        "slug": "edit-exact",
        "dir": "/Users/maks/projects/mark2/bench/tasks/T03-edit-exact",
        "timeout_seconds": 180,
        "repeats": 3,
        "tags": [
          "edit",
          "precision",
          "restraint"
        ],
        "prompt": "The file `bands.py` in the current working directory has one bug.\n\nThe docstring of `classify()` is the specification and it is correct. The code of\n`classify()` disagrees with it for exactly one band boundary, so some\ntemperatures are reported as \"hot\" when the specification says \"mild\".\n\nFix the code so that it matches the docstring.\n\nRules:\n\n- Change only the single wrong value in `classify()`. It is a one-token edit.\n- Do not touch the docstring, the other functions, the constants, the imports,\n  the blank lines or the formatting.\n- Do not rewrite or reformat the file, and do not create new files.\n"
      },
      {
        "id": "T04",
        "slug": "fix-bug-test",
        "dir": "/Users/maks/projects/mark2/bench/tasks/T04-fix-bug-test",
        "timeout_seconds": 240,
        "repeats": 3,
        "tags": [
          "edit",
          "bash",
          "verify-loop"
        ],
        "prompt": "The current working directory contains `stats.py` and `test.py`.\n\nRunning `python3 test.py` currently fails. Make it pass.\n\nRules:\n\n- Fix the bug in `stats.py`. The docstrings in `stats.py` describe the intended\n  behaviour and are correct.\n- Do not modify `test.py` in any way, and do not delete or rename it.\n- Run `python3 test.py` yourself at the end and make sure it prints\n  `all tests passed` and exits successfully.\n"
      },
      {
        "id": "T05",
        "slug": "multi-file-rename",
        "dir": "/Users/maks/projects/mark2/bench/tasks/T05-multi-file-rename",
        "timeout_seconds": 240,
        "repeats": 3,
        "tags": [
          "edit",
          "multi-file",
          "refactor"
        ],
        "prompt": "The current working directory contains two Python files: `lib.py` and `app.py`.\n\nRename the function `calc_total` to `compute_total`.\n\nRules:\n\n- Update every occurrence in both files: the definition, every call site, the\n  `from lib import ...` line, and the mention inside the docstring of\n  `report()` in `app.py`.\n- After the rename, the text `calc_total` must not appear anywhere in either\n  file.\n- Do not rename anything else and do not change any behaviour. Running\n  `python3 app.py` must still print exactly the same output as before.\n- Do not create backup copies of the files.\n"
      },
      {
        "id": "T06",
        "slug": "search-code",
        "dir": "/Users/maks/projects/mark2/bench/tasks/T06-search-code",
        "timeout_seconds": 240,
        "repeats": 3,
        "tags": [
          "search",
          "navigation",
          "reasoning"
        ],
        "prompt": "The directory `src/` in the current working directory contains a small Python\ncodebase (6 files). Explore it and answer one question.\n\nQuestion: which function returns the final amount that a customer is actually\ncharged for an order, and what value does it return for 3 units of SKU `A-11`\nfor a customer in the `gold` tier?\n\nThere is more than one \"total\" function in this codebase; only one of them\nreturns the amount actually charged.\n\nRules:\n\n- Print your answer as the final line of your reply, in exactly this form:\n  `<function_name> = <value>`\n  for example: `some_function = 12.34`\n- Use the plain function name without a module prefix.\n- Give the numeric value the function returns, not a rounded-off guess.\n- Do not modify any file in `src/`.\n"
      },
      {
        "id": "T07",
        "slug": "bash-pipeline",
        "dir": "/Users/maks/projects/mark2/bench/tasks/T07-bash-pipeline",
        "timeout_seconds": 180,
        "repeats": 3,
        "tags": [
          "bash",
          "composition"
        ],
        "prompt": "Count how many lines under the `src/` directory contain the text `TODO`, then\nwrite that count to a file named `report.txt` in the current working directory.\n\nDetails:\n\n- Search `src/` recursively, including subdirectories and non-Python files.\n- Count lines, matching the plain uppercase text `TODO`. Every occurrence in\n  this tree sits on its own line, so the number of matching lines is the number\n  you want.\n- `report.txt` must contain ONLY the number, for example `42`, followed by a\n  newline. No labels, no explanation, no quotes, no other lines.\n- Do not modify anything inside `src/`.\n"
      },
      {
        "id": "T08",
        "slug": "write-run-script",
        "dir": "/Users/maks/projects/mark2/bench/tasks/T08-write-run-script",
        "timeout_seconds": 180,
        "repeats": 3,
        "tags": [
          "write",
          "bash",
          "verify-loop"
        ],
        "prompt": "Write a Python script named `fizzbuzz.py` in the current working directory, then\nrun it and confirm its output.\n\nThe script must print the numbers 1 through 15, one value per line, with these\nsubstitutions:\n\n- print `Fizz` instead of the number when it is divisible by 3\n- print `Buzz` instead of the number when it is divisible by 5\n- print `FizzBuzz` instead of the number when it is divisible by both 3 and 5\n\nSo the expected output is exactly these 15 lines:\n\n```\n1\n2\nFizz\n4\nBuzz\nFizz\n7\n8\nFizz\nBuzz\n11\nFizz\n13\n14\nFizzBuzz\n```\n\nRules:\n\n- The script must be runnable as `python3 fizzbuzz.py` and print nothing else:\n  no header, no trailing summary.\n- It must use only the Python standard library.\n- Run `python3 fizzbuzz.py` yourself and check that the output matches the 15\n  lines above before you finish.\n"
      }
